WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.418
Is Harvard University

00:00:01.418 --> 00:00:03.003
really holding its president?

00:00:03.003 --> 00:00:04.212
Claudine Gay Doctor

00:00:04.212 --> 00:00:06.256
Claudine Gay to the same standards

00:00:06.256 --> 00:00:08.341
when it comes to the plagiarism

00:00:08.341 --> 00:00:09.634
that it would

00:00:09.634 --> 00:00:11.511
assail for students

00:00:11.511 --> 00:00:13.054
committing the same offense.

00:00:13.054 --> 00:00:14.139
You might recall Dr.

00:00:14.139 --> 00:00:16.099
Gay, along with the president of MIT

00:00:16.099 --> 00:00:17.976
and Penn, giving

00:00:17.976 --> 00:00:20.854
generally seen as disastrous testimony

00:00:20.854 --> 00:00:21.855
on Capitol Hill earlier

00:00:21.855 --> 00:00:22.313
this month,

00:00:22.313 --> 00:00:23.356
they failed to

00:00:23.356 --> 00:00:24.858
explicitly say that calling

00:00:24.858 --> 00:00:26.985
for the genocide of Jews on campus

00:00:26.985 --> 00:00:28.236
would violate their campuses

00:00:28.236 --> 00:00:29.237
codes of conduct

00:00:29.237 --> 00:00:30.989
that, as well as gays commitment

00:00:30.989 --> 00:00:32.198
to progressive policies

00:00:32.198 --> 00:00:33.450
have made her many right

00:00:33.450 --> 00:00:35.285
wing enemies who have

00:00:35.285 --> 00:00:36.494
recently raised issues

00:00:36.494 --> 00:00:37.704
about her scholarship.

00:00:37.704 --> 00:00:39.789
But regardless of the provenance

00:00:39.789 --> 00:00:41.499
of these allegations,

00:00:41.499 --> 00:00:42.459
there is also the matter

00:00:42.459 --> 00:00:44.961
of whether or not they're true.

00:00:44.961 --> 00:00:46.004
Harvard's top governing

00:00:46.004 --> 00:00:47.088
body said a review

00:00:47.088 --> 00:00:48.298
revealed, quote,

00:00:48.298 --> 00:00:50.258
Inadequate citations by Dr.

00:00:50.258 --> 00:00:52.469
Gay in a few instances, but quote,

00:00:52.469 --> 00:00:54.220
No violation of Harvard standards

00:00:54.220 --> 00:00:56.431
for research misconduct, unquote.

00:00:56.431 --> 00:00:56.973
The Harvard

00:00:56.973 --> 00:00:58.433
Guide on Sourcing

00:00:58.433 --> 00:01:00.727
says this on plagiarism, quote,

00:01:00.727 --> 00:01:01.728
In academic writing,

00:01:01.728 --> 00:01:02.353
it is considered

00:01:02.353 --> 00:01:03.313
plagiarism to draw

00:01:03.313 --> 00:01:05.482
any idea or any language

00:01:05.482 --> 00:01:06.357
from someone else

00:01:06.357 --> 00:01:07.901
without adequately crediting

00:01:07.901 --> 00:01:09.736
that source in your paper, unquote.

00:01:09.736 --> 00:01:10.445
Critics of Dr.

00:01:10.445 --> 00:01:11.863
Gay and Harvard's

00:01:11.863 --> 00:01:13.323
review of the allegations say that

00:01:13.323 --> 00:01:16.534
there is a double standard going on here.

00:01:16.618 --> 00:01:18.620
CNN's Matt Egan is following this.

00:01:18.620 --> 00:01:19.871
Matt, what exactly is

00:01:19.871 --> 00:01:21.915
Doctor Gay accused of here.

00:01:21.915 --> 00:01:23.333
Which including Gay's career,

00:01:23.333 --> 00:01:24.542
is under a microscope

00:01:24.542 --> 00:01:25.627
and now she's facing

00:01:25.627 --> 00:01:28.004
facing accusations of plagiarism.

00:01:28.004 --> 00:01:28.922
Now, Gay recently

00:01:28.922 --> 00:01:30.924
submitted corrections to two papers

00:01:30.924 --> 00:01:32.425
she wrote as a professional academic

00:01:32.425 --> 00:01:34.594
in 2001 and 2017.

00:01:34.594 --> 00:01:36.346
However, there are clear

00:01:36.346 --> 00:01:37.806
examples of plagiarism

00:01:37.806 --> 00:01:39.390
that occurred in the 1990s

00:01:39.390 --> 00:01:41.392
when Gay was studying for her Ph.D.

00:01:41.392 --> 00:01:42.352
at Harvard.

00:01:42.352 --> 00:01:43.394
And when one example,

00:01:43.394 --> 00:01:44.771
Gay's 1997

00:01:44.771 --> 00:01:46.815
dissertation lifted one paragraph

00:01:46.815 --> 00:01:48.066
almost verbatim

00:01:48.066 --> 00:01:49.109
from another,

00:01:49.109 --> 00:01:51.277
from another source without citation.

00:01:51.277 --> 00:01:51.986
That offense

00:01:51.986 --> 00:01:53.947
appears to go against Harvard's

00:01:53.947 --> 00:01:55.448
current guide on plagiarism,

00:01:55.448 --> 00:01:57.075
which you stated earlier, Jake.

00:01:57.075 --> 00:01:58.868
Harvard's Plager is and policy

00:01:58.868 --> 00:01:59.661
says that students

00:01:59.661 --> 00:02:02.664
who submit work without clear attribution

00:02:02.705 --> 00:02:04.124
to sources will be, quote,

00:02:04.124 --> 00:02:05.875
subject to disciplinary action

00:02:05.875 --> 00:02:07.919
up to and including requirement

00:02:07.919 --> 00:02:09.879
to withdraw from the college.

00:02:09.879 --> 00:02:11.506
Now, the first publicly identified

00:02:11.506 --> 00:02:12.549
instance of plagiarism

00:02:12.549 --> 00:02:14.509
by gay comes from a failure

00:02:14.509 --> 00:02:18.221
to properly cite sources in a 1993 essay.

00:02:18.304 --> 00:02:19.639
These instances of plagiarism

00:02:19.639 --> 00:02:20.431
were first reported

00:02:20.431 --> 00:02:22.016
by the Washington Free Beacon.

00:02:22.016 --> 00:02:24.018
CNN's analysis, led by my colleague

00:02:24.018 --> 00:02:25.395
and Stack, confirmed

00:02:25.395 --> 00:02:26.896
some of the main allegations

00:02:26.896 --> 00:02:28.106
in that reporting.

00:02:28.106 --> 00:02:29.607
Now, Harvard's top governing

00:02:29.607 --> 00:02:31.151
body said in a statement last week

00:02:31.151 --> 00:02:33.736
it became aware of plagiarism allegations

00:02:33.736 --> 00:02:36.072
against gay in late October.

00:02:36.072 --> 00:02:37.323
An independent review

00:02:37.323 --> 00:02:38.533
found a few instances

00:02:38.533 --> 00:02:39.701
of missing citations,

00:02:39.701 --> 00:02:42.287
but no violation of Harvard standards.

00:02:42.287 --> 00:02:43.913
However, it's not clear

00:02:43.913 --> 00:02:45.790
whether that review included

00:02:45.790 --> 00:02:48.042
Gay's 1997 dissertation.

00:02:48.042 --> 00:02:49.419
Now, neither Harvard nor Gay

00:02:49.419 --> 00:02:50.128
have commented

00:02:50.128 --> 00:02:51.087
on the allegations

00:02:51.087 --> 00:02:53.548
of plagiarism from the 1990s.

00:02:53.548 --> 00:02:54.674
CNN reached out to Harvard

00:02:54.674 --> 00:02:55.675
with a list of questions,

00:02:55.675 --> 00:02:57.760
but the school declined to comment.

00:02:57.760 --> 00:03:00.430
In a previous statement, Gay said, quote,

00:03:00.430 --> 00:03:02.473
I stand by the integrity

00:03:02.473 --> 00:03:03.433
of my scholarship.

00:03:03.433 --> 00:03:04.392
Throughout my career,

00:03:04.392 --> 00:03:05.977
I have worked to ensure

00:03:05.977 --> 00:03:07.395
my scholarship adheres

00:03:07.395 --> 00:03:09.397
to the highest academic standards.

00:03:09.397 --> 00:03:11.232
Now, we should note that the plagiarism

00:03:11.232 --> 00:03:12.233
allegations against Gay

00:03:12.233 --> 00:03:13.151
were first circulated

00:03:13.151 --> 00:03:15.195
by conservative activist activists.

00:03:15.195 --> 00:03:16.321
One of those activists

00:03:16.321 --> 00:03:17.739
has also criticized gay

00:03:17.739 --> 00:03:19.657
and Harvard's diversity policies

00:03:19.657 --> 00:03:21.826
and Gay's most outspoken critic.

00:03:21.826 --> 00:03:22.243
Hedge fund

00:03:22.243 --> 00:03:23.077
billionaire Bill

00:03:23.077 --> 00:03:24.495
Ackman has also highlighted

00:03:24.495 --> 00:03:25.496
the plagiarism charges,

00:03:25.496 --> 00:03:27.999
and he's argued without evidence

00:03:27.999 --> 00:03:28.666
that Harvard

00:03:28.666 --> 00:03:29.876
only hired gay

00:03:29.876 --> 00:03:32.170
to fulfill diversity requirements.

00:03:32.170 --> 00:03:33.004
Now, plagiarism

00:03:33.004 --> 00:03:34.547
experts that CNN spoke to stressed

00:03:34.547 --> 00:03:36.883
that this is a very complex issue.

00:03:36.883 --> 00:03:38.343
These experts were divided on

00:03:38.343 --> 00:03:39.928
whether Gay's omissions warrant

00:03:39.928 --> 00:03:40.970
any punishment,

00:03:40.970 --> 00:03:41.971
but none of them called

00:03:41.971 --> 00:03:43.181
for her to be fired.

00:03:43.181 --> 00:03:44.015
And they noted it's

00:03:44.015 --> 00:03:45.558
quite rare for academics

00:03:45.558 --> 00:03:47.227
to be fired for plagiarism.

00:03:47.227 --> 00:03:47.644
Jake,

00:03:47.644 --> 00:03:49.145
what else are critics and plagiarism

00:03:49.145 --> 00:03:51.064
experts saying about Harvard's

00:03:51.064 --> 00:03:54.067
review of these plagiarism allegations?

00:03:54.192 --> 00:03:54.817
Well, one

00:03:54.817 --> 00:03:56.527
criticism is the lightning fast

00:03:56.527 --> 00:03:57.904
speed that Harvard pulled off

00:03:57.904 --> 00:03:59.197
this independent review.

00:03:59.197 --> 00:04:01.741
These types of plagiarism reviews,

00:04:01.741 --> 00:04:03.785
they can take anywhere from six months

00:04:03.785 --> 00:04:04.869
to two years.

00:04:04.869 --> 00:04:07.205
This one, not even two months.

00:04:07.205 --> 00:04:08.248
Another criticism

00:04:08.248 --> 00:04:09.666
is that there's a double standard,

00:04:09.666 --> 00:04:11.960
one set of rules for the students.

00:04:11.960 --> 00:04:13.836
Another for the president

00:04:13.836 --> 00:04:15.046
of the university.

00:04:15.046 --> 00:04:16.297
I do think we should emphasize,

00:04:16.297 --> 00:04:18.299
though, that the defense

00:04:18.299 --> 00:04:20.385
that these flames have been fired

00:04:20.385 --> 00:04:22.178
and fueled by activists,

00:04:22.178 --> 00:04:23.054
not activists,

00:04:23.054 --> 00:04:24.597
who are really concerned

00:04:24.597 --> 00:04:27.100
about academic citation methods,

00:04:27.100 --> 00:04:28.977
but right wing activists

00:04:28.977 --> 00:04:30.478
who are trying to push out

00:04:30.478 --> 00:04:32.105
the presidents of Ivy League schools

00:04:32.105 --> 00:04:34.399
who they feel are too liberal.

00:04:34.399 --> 00:04:36.234
One last point here, Jake.

00:04:36.234 --> 00:04:37.694
Remember, when you talk about plagiarism,

00:04:37.694 --> 00:04:38.194
it really falls

00:04:38.194 --> 00:04:40.363
into two different categories.

00:04:40.363 --> 00:04:42.865
One is copying without attribution.

00:04:42.865 --> 00:04:44.826
There's also the more serious allegation

00:04:44.826 --> 00:04:47.161
of stealing someone else's ideas.

00:04:47.161 --> 00:04:48.830
And in this instance,

00:04:48.830 --> 00:04:50.707
these allegations of plagiarism

00:04:50.707 --> 00:04:52.417
are really all in the former camp.

00:04:52.417 --> 00:04:53.293
So, yes,

00:04:53.293 --> 00:04:56.045
sloppy citations, but no, not idea.

00:04:56.045 --> 00:04:57.005
Theft. Yes.

00:04:57.005 --> 00:04:58.506
The big question, I think, in

00:04:58.506 --> 00:04:59.007
the future

00:04:59.007 --> 00:05:02.218
is how will Harvard be able to punish

00:05:02.218 --> 00:05:04.220
any students

00:05:04.304 --> 00:05:05.096
found guilty of the

00:05:05.096 --> 00:05:10.059
same offense without

00:05:10.143 --> 00:05:11.728
inviting a lawsuit?

00:05:11.728 --> 00:05:14.605
Because if she gets away with something

00:05:14.605 --> 00:05:16.858
that students can't get away with,

00:05:16.858 --> 00:05:19.110
that could be messy

00:05:19.110 --> 00:05:20.236
legally for the school,

00:05:20.236 --> 00:05:21.988
which I'm sure Harvard's lawyers

00:05:21.988 --> 00:05:23.281
have thought about way before.

00:05:23.281 --> 00:05:24.240
I just said that.

00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:25.450
Madigan, Thank you so much.

00:05:25.450 --> 00:05:26.117
Really appreciate it.

